   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "## Task 6 — Real-world Questions"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "print(\"\"\"\n",
    "══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n",
    "Q1. Why does Bag of Words fail to understand semantic meaning?\n",
    "══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n",
    "\n",
    "BoW treats each word as an independent token.  It has no notion of:\n",
    "\n",
    "  (a) Synonymy — words with the same meaning:\n",
    "        "happy"   vs "glad"   vs "joyful"   vs "pleased"\n",
    "        "awful"   vs "terrible" vs "dreadful" vs "horrible"\n",
    "      BoW produces entirely different vectors for these sentences\n",
    "      even though they convey the identical sentiment.\n",
    "\n",
    "  (b) Polysemy — one word with multiple meanings:\n",
    "        "I like this bank"  (financial institution vs river bank)\n",
    "        BoW uses the same feature for both senses.\n",
    "\n",
    "  (c) Word order / syntax:\n",
    "        "The food was not good" vs "The food was good"\n",
    "        Both share mostly the same BoW features despite opposite meaning.\n",
    "\n",
    "  (d) Context / compositionality:\n",
    "        "killer app" (great application) vs "killer\" (murderer).\n",
    "\n",
    "Example from our dataset:\n",
    "  Review A: \"This product is excellent\"     → [excellent:1, product:1, ...]\n",
    "  Review B: \"This item is superb\"           → [superb:1, item:1, ...]\n",
    "  Cosine similarity of BoW vectors ≈ 0.0   (no shared content words!)\n",
    "  But both reviews express the same positive sentiment.\n",
    "\n",
    "Solution: Dense word embeddings (Word2Vec, GloVe, BERT) encode semantic\n",
    "proximity — synonyms end up close in the embedding space.\n",
    "\"\"\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "print(\"\"\"\n",
    "══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n",
    "Q2. When to use Bag of Words vs TF-IDF in industry?\n",
    "══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n",
    "\n",
    "┌─────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n",
    "│ Use BoW when…               │ Use TF-IDF when…                             │\n",
    "├─────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n",
    "│ Corpus is small & clean     │ Corpus is large or noisy                     │\n",
    "│ All words equally relevant  │ Rare terms are important discriminators      │\n",
    "│ Naive Bayes baseline needed │ Information retrieval / search ranking       │\n",
    "│ Fast, interpretable feature │ Document similarity or clustering            │\n",
    "│ Very short texts (tweets)   │ Longer documents (articles, reviews)         │\n",
    "│ Domain vocab is controlled  │ Generic, mixed-domain corpora                │\n",
    "└─────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n",
    "\n",
    "Industry examples:\n",
    "  • Email spam detection (Naive Bayes + BoW) — simple, fast, works well.\n",
    "  • Search engines (TF-IDF) — ranks documents by query term importance.\n",
    "  • Content recommendation — TF-IDF to find similar articles.\n",
    "  • Sentiment analysis — TF-IDF often outperforms BoW on varied vocabulary.\n",
    "\"\"\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "print(\"\"\"\n",
    "══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n",
    "Q3. Limitations of TF-IDF in real applications\n",
    "══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n",
    "\n",
    "1. No semantic understanding:\n",
    "   Like BoW, TF-IDF is bag-of-words at heart — synonyms, context,\n",
    "   and word order are ignored.\n",
    "\n",
    "2. Vocabulary mismatch (OOV):\n",
    "   Words unseen during training are silently dropped.  Slang, typos,\n",
    "   or domain jargon in production data cause silent degradation.\n",
    "\n",
    "3. Corpus dependency:\n",
    "   IDF values are specific to the training corpus.  A model trained on\n",
    "   news data will have wrong IDF weights when applied to legal text.\n",
    "\n",
    "4. Long document bias:\n",
    "   Without length normalisation, long documents score higher simply\n",
    "   because they mention terms more often.  (sklearn normalises by default.)\n",
    "\n",
    "5. Does not capture multi-word expressions:\n",
    "   "not good" is treated as ["not", "good"] — both potentially positive\n",
    "   unigrams — rather than a negative bigram.  (Mitigated by n-grams.)\n",
    "\n",
    "6. Sparse, high-dimensional vectors:\n",
    "   At large vocabulary sizes, training deep models on TF-IDF vectors\n",
    "   is expensive and often worse than dense embeddings.\n",
    "\n",
    "7. No positional or structural information:\n",
    "   TF-IDF does not distinguish whether a term appears in the title,\n",
    "   opening sentence, or a footnote.\n",
    "\"\"\")"
   ]
