    "---\n",
    "## Summary & Observations\n",
    "\n",
    "| Technique | Strengths | Weaknesses |\n",
    "|-----------|-----------|------------|\n",
    "| **One-Hot Encoding** | Simple, binary, interpretable | No frequency info, very large vectors |\n",
    "| **Bag of Words** | Captures frequency, fast baseline | No semantic meaning, order ignored |\n",
    "| **TF-IDF** | Rewards discriminating terms, good for IR | Still no semantics, corpus-dependent |\n",
    "\n",
    "### Key Findings\n",
    "1. **Vocabulary size**: After preprocessing ~122 reviews, the vocabulary settled at ~600-800 unique tokens — manageable here, but explodes on larger corpora.\n",
    "2. **Sparsity**: All three matrices are >90% sparse, confirming that sparse storage (CSR format) is essential at scale.\n",
    "3. **Sentiment classification**: TF-IDF generally gives equal or better accuracy than raw BoW because it down-weights ubiquitous words (\"product\", \"quality\") and highlights discriminating terms (\"excellent\", \"terrible\", \"broken\").\n",
    "4. **Top discriminating words**: Logistic Regression coefficients reveal intuitive polarity — positive side shows "excellent", "love", "recommend"; negative side shows "broken", "terrible", "useless", "avoid".\n",
    "5. **Semantic gap**: BoW/TF-IDF treat synonyms (e.g. "awful" ≠ "terrible") as unrelated features — a key motivation for moving to word embeddings or transformers (BERT) in production."
   ]
